1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,248
The school next joins toxidrome recognition with acute kidney injury.
We will connect the visible pattern to the immediate threat, then
explain why poisoning, heat and poor perfusion can injure several
systems together.

2
00:00:13,248 --> 00:00:24,024
A toxidrome helps organize findings when the agent is uncertain. It
does not replace glucose, neurological assessment, trauma checks or
effective basic support.

3
00:00:24,024 --> 00:00:36,480
For renal illness, think about incoming perfusion, injury within the
kidney and outgoing urine drainage. The mechanisms may overlap and do
not all call for the same fluid treatment.

4
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:49,032
Use these original cases to rehearse an explanation and a next action.
The named school sections remain the spine, with the Nancy reading and
current local authorization kept visible.

5
00:00:49,032 --> 00:01:01,512
The same substance can produce different findings depending on the
route, time course, formulation and patient. A sustained-release
preparation can behave differently from an immediate-release tablet.

6
00:01:01,512 --> 00:01:12,360
Absorption may still be occurring when you arrive. A patient who
initially talks normally can deteriorate later, so a single
comfortable appearance cannot establish safety.

7
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:25,440
Co-exposures and prescribed medicines can blur a classic pattern. Ask
about alcohol, sedating medicines and other agents without assuming
that a reported substance is the only one present.

8
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:37,128
Record the reported amount and its uncertainty. A street tablet or a
hit is not a standardized concentration, so a unit count does not
establish a reliable dose.

9
00:01:37,128 --> 00:01:49,848
Both sympathetic and parasympathetic preganglionic neurons use
acetylcholine at nicotinic receptors in autonomic ganglia.
Acetylcholine is not limited to the parasympathetic branch.

10
00:01:49,848 --> 00:02:02,904
Most parasympathetic target effects use muscarinic receptors. Most
sympathetic target nerves release norepinephrine, while sympathetic
sweating pathways use acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors.

11
00:02:02,904 --> 00:02:14,544
Nicotinic receptors also transmit from motor nerves to skeletal
muscle. This explains why cholinergic poisoning can cause both
secretions and muscle weakness.

12
00:02:14,544 --> 00:02:25,680
These distinctions correct an oversimplified sympathetic-versus-
parasympathetic split. Use the receptor effects to predict the
breathing and circulation problem you need to manage.

13
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:38,928
Begin with whether the patient can protect the airway and breathe
effectively. Then compare mental status, secretions, skin, pupils,
muscle activity, pulse, pressure and temperature.

14
00:02:38,928 --> 00:02:51,096
Dry delirium suggests antimuscarinic effects; wet secretions and
weakness suggest cholinergic excess. Sweating with marked arousal
suggests a sympathomimetic pattern.

15
00:02:51,096 --> 00:03:03,168
Depressed consciousness with slow, shallow breathing suggests opioid
or another depressant effect. Small pupils add evidence but are not a
required condition for ventilation support.

16
00:03:03,168 --> 00:03:14,232
These are probabilities, not unique chemical fingerprints. An
incomplete pattern should increase careful reassessment rather than
delay treatment of a visible life threat.

17
00:03:14,232 --> 00:03:25,632
Muscarinic blockade reduces normal secretions and can impair sweating.
The patient may have dry mouth and skin, flushing, dilated pupils and
rising temperature.

18
00:03:25,632 --> 00:03:37,032
Central effects can include confusion, hallucinations or delirium. A
frightened, disoriented patient needs a calm approach and repeated
physiological assessment.

19
00:03:37,032 --> 00:03:48,552
Peripheral effects include tachycardia, urinary retention and reduced
bowel activity. Ask about the last normal void and recent medication
changes when practical.

20
00:03:48,552 --> 00:04:01,632
The classic dry-hot-delirious cluster is useful, but no single pupil
size, heart rate or skin finding proves the agent. Continue to check
glucose, temperature and competing causes.

21
00:04:01,632 --> 00:04:13,848
Some over-the-counter antihistamines and prescribed medicines have
antimuscarinic properties. More than one product can contribute,
including combination cold or sleep preparations.

22
00:04:13,848 --> 00:04:27,000
Tricyclic antidepressant toxicity also blocks fast cardiac sodium
channels. A dry, confused patient can therefore develop conduction
delay, seizures and profound hypotension.

23
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:38,160
Look at the rhythm and QRS as well as the skin. A widening complex or
deteriorating perfusion is a reason to request advanced support and
prepare for rapid decline.

24
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:50,712
Current local bicarbonate treatment belongs to an advanced,
consultation-led pathway. Recognizing its purpose does not authorize
an independent PCP bicarbonate or antiarrhythmic recipe.

25
00:04:50,712 --> 00:05:02,328
Acetylcholinesterase inhibition can allow acetylcholine to accumulate.
Both muscarinic and nicotinic effects may occur, with variable heart
rate and pupil findings.

26
00:05:02,328 --> 00:05:18,312
SLUDGE-M recalls salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation,
gastrointestinal upset, emesis and miosis. Add the three dangerous Bs:
bronchorrhea, bronchospasm and bradycardia.

27
00:05:18,312 --> 00:05:31,608
Profuse bronchial secretions and narrowed airways can obstruct
effective breathing. Suction and effective ventilation, once contact
is safe, matter more than reciting the mnemonic perfectly.

28
00:05:31,608 --> 00:05:45,048
A wet patient is not automatically a pesticide diagnosis. Pulmonary
edema, aspiration and other disease remain possible, and exposure
safety must be established before contact.

29
00:05:45,048 --> 00:05:55,488
Nicotinic overstimulation can progress from twitching to weakness and
paralysis. A quieter patient may be losing muscle function rather than
recovering.

30
00:05:55,488 --> 00:06:07,056
Assess breathing depth, chest movement and air entry, not just a
respiratory count. Respiratory muscle failure can coexist with
secretions and bronchospasm.

31
00:06:07,056 --> 00:06:19,824
Atropine reverses muscarinic effects but does not correct the
nicotinic muscle problem. The current public J zero five places
atropine at ACP and pralidoxime at CCP level.

32
00:06:19,824 --> 00:06:33,672
For an ordinary PCP, the actionable plan is exposure-safe basic
support, required consultation and early advanced help. A school
phrase saying if in scope does not supply a missing authorization.

33
00:06:33,672 --> 00:06:45,888
Sympathomimetic agents stimulate adrenergic activity directly or
increase catecholamine availability. Examples include cocaine,
amphetamines and some other stimulants.

34
00:06:45,888 --> 00:06:58,704
Agitation, dilated pupils, rapid pulse and high pressure overlap
antimuscarinic toxicity. Marked sweating is a useful comparison clue,
although mixed exposures can blur it.

35
00:06:58,704 --> 00:07:12,264
Danger extends beyond behaviour: assess for chest discomfort, rhythm
changes, temperature elevation, seizures and impaired perfusion. Young
age does not exclude serious cardiac injury.

36
00:07:12,264 --> 00:07:23,592
Sustained muscle activity and heat can contribute to rhabdomyolysis,
potassium release and kidney injury. This is one bridge between the
two school sections.

37
00:07:23,592 --> 00:07:35,424
Use distance, an exit plan and a calm, simple explanation. Reduce
noise and unnecessary confrontation while arranging the resources
needed for safe assessment.

38
00:07:35,424 --> 00:07:49,920
Agitation can reflect hypoxia, low glucose, infection, neurological
disease or heat as well as intoxication. Measure what is safe and
immediately useful, including temperature and glucose.

39
00:07:49,920 --> 00:08:03,192
Limit avoidable exertion and use the established heat-illness cooling
process when indicated. Do not restrain a patient prone or compromise
breathing in order to obtain a tidy set of observations.

40
00:08:03,192 --> 00:08:16,632
Medication-assisted control requires its actual authorized team and
monitoring. Once the patient becomes quieter, immediately reassess
airway, ventilation, temperature and perfusion.

41
00:08:16,632 --> 00:08:29,400
Opioid receptor agonism depresses respiratory drive and consciousness.
The important observation is whether enough air is reaching the lungs,
not simply whether the patient looks asleep.

42
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:39,576
Count the rate and evaluate depth, air movement and chest rise.
Snoring or occasional gasps do not establish adequate ventilation.

43
00:08:39,576 --> 00:08:50,424
Small pupils can support the pattern, but hypoxia, mixed exposure or
another condition can change that finding. Do not withhold support
while waiting for pinpoint pupils.

44
00:08:50,424 --> 00:09:01,680
Fentanyl-related rigidity can occasionally complicate ventilation.
Difficulty ventilating requires prompt troubleshooting and advanced
help rather than repeated forceful bagging.

45
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:13,920
For inadequate breathing with a pulse, establish an open airway, clear
secretions and use the indicated adjunct. Obtain a two-provider mask
seal when resources allow.

46
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:26,520
Give controlled breaths through the appropriate resuscitation pathway,
watching for chest rise and allowing exhalation. Check the mask,
position and obstruction before increasing force.

47
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:38,424
Supplemental oxygen can preserve a saturation reading while carbon
dioxide rises from hypoventilation. A reassuring oxygen number cannot
replace the breathing examination.

48
00:09:38,424 --> 00:09:49,584
If there is no definite pulse and the arrest branch applies,
prioritize CPR and ventilation. Naloxone must not interrupt the
required resuscitation sequence.

49
00:09:49,584 --> 00:10:00,144
Naloxone competitively blocks opioid effects at receptors. Give it for
the indicated suspected opioid respiratory problem while effective
ventilation continues.

50
00:10:00,144 --> 00:10:10,272
The treatment endpoint is adequate spontaneous breathing. Full
alertness, anger or withdrawal is not the required sign that the
treatment has worked.

51
00:10:10,272 --> 00:10:20,592
Allow the indicated time for the route to work and reassess before
repeating. Count any doses already given by the patient, companions or
other responders.

52
00:10:20,592 --> 00:10:33,840
Palliative and prescribed-opioid cases need careful pain and
withdrawal planning with the actual clinical team. Their separate
titration instructions must not be blended with a generic overdose
schedule.

53
00:10:33,840 --> 00:10:46,920
The currently checked adult PCP monograph lists point four milligrams,
point four, point eight, then two milligrams by the authorized IM or
IV route, every three minutes as required.

54
00:10:46,920 --> 00:10:59,280
This is not point four repeated four times. The fourth IV dose is
preferred in the source, and further doses require CliniCall
consultation rather than an improvised continuation.

55
00:10:59,280 --> 00:11:12,192
Reassess respiratory function and prior treatment at every step. The
schedule is a maximum sequence of opportunities, not an instruction to
finish every dose after breathing has improved.

56
00:11:12,192 --> 00:11:24,168
Verify the actual product concentration and calculate the volume
separately. This adult schedule does not supply a neonatal, pediatric,
nasal-device or palliative dosing order.

57
00:11:24,168 --> 00:11:36,312
The current monograph includes a metered intranasal option in the
first-responder pathway. A labelled nasal device is not
interchangeable with an arbitrary volume from an injectable ampoule.

58
00:11:36,312 --> 00:11:48,576
Use the actual supplied device instructions, authorized dose and
repeat interval. Do not ask an unresponsive patient to inhale
deliberately as a condition for giving a permitted nasal product.

59
00:11:48,576 --> 00:11:59,232
Nasal medication is absorbed through mucosa, while perfusion and local
conditions affect delivery. It does not replace assisted ventilation
in an apneic patient.

60
00:11:59,232 --> 00:12:10,944
Check whether someone already used a nasal product and at what time.
Communicate that treatment as part of the total history rather than
resetting the sequence at ambulance arrival.

61
00:12:10,944 --> 00:12:23,592
Sedative-hypnotic drugs and alcohol can reduce awareness, coordination
and respiratory function. Pupil findings and circulation vary,
especially when several substances are involved.

62
00:12:23,592 --> 00:12:36,600
If breathing improves after naloxone but the patient remains drowsy,
continue to protect the airway and investigate other causes. Chasing
full consciousness with repeated naloxone can add harm.

63
00:12:36,600 --> 00:12:50,040
Flumazenil can provoke seizures in selected high-risk or mixed
exposures and is a CCP medication in the current local source. It is
not a routine PCP response to an unknown sedated patient.

64
00:12:50,040 --> 00:13:01,944
Maintain a broad differential, including trauma, glucose disturbance,
stroke, infection and hypoxia. A substance history should add evidence
without closing the assessment.

65
00:13:01,944 --> 00:13:15,216
Small pupils with shallow breathing suggest one priority, while small
pupils with copious secretions and fasciculations suggest another. In
both, breathing support may be immediately necessary.

66
00:13:15,216 --> 00:13:27,936
A fast pulse and agitation can also result from low glucose, heat,
infection or hypoxia. Measure glucose and temperature and assess
actual ventilation and perfusion.

67
00:13:27,936 --> 00:13:39,048
A reported overdose does not exclude a fall or a stroke. Look for
injury, focal findings, last known well and a course that does not fit
the proposed explanation.

68
00:13:39,048 --> 00:13:48,888
State the working pattern and the serious alternatives. Then choose
actions that address present threats without requiring certainty about
the exact chemical.

69
00:13:48,888 --> 00:14:01,296
A chemical exposure is not simply an ordinary medical patient wearing
unusual clothing. Residual material can injure responders, contaminate
the ambulance and close receiving facilities.

70
00:14:01,296 --> 00:14:15,048
Current J zero one and PR zero five keep BCEHS care out of hot and
warm zones and require consultation for exposure and decontamination
planning. Do not enter because you recognize the toxidrome.

71
00:14:15,048 --> 00:14:28,776
Work with the designated teams on an agent-specific process and
confirm appropriate decontamination before loading. Provide clear
explanations, privacy, assistance and protection from heat loss.

72
00:14:28,776 --> 00:14:42,072
Do not induce vomiting or improvise neutralization or oral dilution. A
caustic ingestion also changes airway choices: the current local
source excludes supraglottic devices in that situation.

73
00:14:42,072 --> 00:14:56,448
Ask what happened using open, nonjudgmental questions. Distinguish
accidental dosing error, recreational exposure, occupational exposure
and possible intentional self-harm without presuming motivation.

74
00:14:56,448 --> 00:15:09,072
Identify the agent, formulation, route and time when possible. A
product label or prescribed supply count may help, but missing tablets
do not prove that all were swallowed.

75
00:15:09,072 --> 00:15:20,472
Use safe information or an appropriate label image rather than
carrying a leaking or contaminated container into the ambulance. Do
not sniff an unknown substance to identify it.

76
00:15:20,472 --> 00:15:33,672
Record observed findings separately from reported history and include
previous treatment. Possible self-harm also needs a safe,
compassionate mental-health assessment and the appropriate care
pathway.

77
00:15:33,672 --> 00:15:47,112
A forty-six-year-old has taken several cold and sleep products and is
now confused, flushed and dry. Pupils are large, pulse is one hundred
twenty-eight and temperature is thirty-nine point three.

78
00:15:47,112 --> 00:15:59,760
The pattern suggests antimuscarinic effects, but the products and
amounts remain uncertain. Check glucose, breathing, injury and the
medication history rather than diagnosing from dryness alone.

79
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:11,616
A usable tracing shows a widening QRS. This adds a potentially
dangerous cardiac effect and calls for urgent consultation, advanced
resources and conveyance.

80
00:16:11,616 --> 00:16:22,680
Treat the current physiological threats and heat through the
authorized pathway. A later quieter appearance does not establish
recovery if ventilation or pressure is worsening.

81
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:35,136
At a greenhouse spill, the designated team controls the contaminated
area. You arrange required consultation and receive the patient only
when the contact and decontamination plan is safe.

82
00:16:35,136 --> 00:16:47,808
The patient has salivation, tearing, wheeze and heavy bronchial
secretions, followed by muscle twitching and weak shallow breaths.
This fits a concerning cholinergic pattern.

83
00:16:47,808 --> 00:16:59,520
After safe access, support airway clearance and effective ventilation
while requesting advanced resources. Do not wait for a particular
heart rate or every item of SLUDGE-M.

84
00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:11,880
Explain the distinction in handover: secretions threaten airway and
airflow, while nicotinic weakness threatens the respiratory pump.
Treating one does not guarantee that the other has resolved.

85
00:17:11,880 --> 00:17:23,808
A twenty-nine-year-old is unresponsive and breathing shallowly five
times per minute. After oxygen is applied, the saturation improves,
but chest movement remains poor.

86
00:17:23,808 --> 00:17:35,208
This is still inadequate ventilation. Open and clear the airway,
obtain an effective seal and provide assisted breaths while a partner
prepares indicated naloxone.

87
00:17:35,208 --> 00:17:49,296
After treatment, spontaneous depth and rate improve although the
patient remains drowsy. Continue monitoring and assess mixed exposure,
injury and glucose rather than chasing complete wakefulness.

88
00:17:49,296 --> 00:18:01,800
Plan conveyance and watch for recurrent depression. If the person
later declines care, use the actual consent and refusal process rather
than calling one response a safe-discharge test.

89
00:18:01,800 --> 00:18:13,944
The kidneys filter plasma and then selectively reabsorb or secrete
substances along the nephron. Urine is the final output of that
regulated process, not a direct copy of the blood.

90
00:18:13,944 --> 00:18:26,304
Kidneys regulate water, sodium, potassium and acid-base balance. Acute
loss of function can therefore produce both retained waste and life-
threatening physiological disturbance.

91
00:18:26,304 --> 00:18:40,440
Fluid overload can worsen breathing; potassium disturbance can impair
cardiac conduction; acidosis can change respiratory demand. Confusion
or weakness may be part of the presentation.

92
00:18:40,440 --> 00:18:52,680
Kidney dysfunction can change medicine clearance and adverse-effect
risk. A standard dose or familiar pain medicine is not automatically
suitable for every patient with suspected injury.

93
00:18:52,680 --> 00:19:05,616
Prerenal describes reduced effective perfusion reaching the kidney
before primary tissue injury has become the main problem. It is a
functional mechanism, not a claim that the patient must look
dehydrated.

94
00:19:05,616 --> 00:19:18,072
Intrinsic injury affects structures within the kidney, including
vessels, glomeruli, tubules or interstitial tissue. Causes include
ischemia, inflammation and toxic injury.

95
00:19:18,072 --> 00:19:31,344
Postrenal obstruction impedes urine leaving the kidney and can raise
pressure upstream. The outflow problem needs identification and
definitive relief, not simply more fluid entering the circulation.

96
00:19:31,344 --> 00:19:42,144
These categories can coexist. Prolonged low perfusion can damage
tubules, while sepsis may involve both flow abnormalities and
intrinsic injury.

97
00:19:42,144 --> 00:19:53,040
Vomiting, diarrhea, hemorrhage and poor intake can reduce circulating
volume and renal perfusion. The urine history may add a clue to the
wider deterioration.

98
00:19:53,040 --> 00:20:05,448
Sepsis can reduce effective perfusion through tone, leakage and
microcirculatory changes. It can also cause intrinsic injury, so one
category is not the entire explanation.

99
00:20:05,448 --> 00:20:16,416
Heart failure can impair renal perfusion while edema is present. Low
output and congestion make the fluid decision different from
uncomplicated volume depletion.

100
00:20:16,416 --> 00:20:28,776
A low urine report should prompt assessment of the whole circulation,
not an automatic bolus. Early treatment of the cause can prevent
prolonged hypoperfusion from becoming structural injury.

101
00:20:28,776 --> 00:20:40,176
Reduced renal perfusion can activate renin release and the
angiotensin-aldosterone system. Angiotensin contributes to
vasoconstriction and stimulates aldosterone.

102
00:20:40,176 --> 00:20:50,664
Aldosterone promotes sodium retention and potassium secretion in
appropriate nephron segments, with water balance affected alongside
other hormones and intake.

103
00:20:50,664 --> 00:21:03,240
This response helps defend effective circulation but cannot stop
hemorrhage, reverse a toxin or remove an obstruction. Preserved
pressure may be compensation rather than adequate organ flow.

104
00:21:03,240 --> 00:21:15,624
NSAIDs and renin-angiotensin medicines can alter renal responses
during acute illness. Record them for clinical review without
instructing the patient to permanently stop a beneficial prescription.

105
00:21:15,624 --> 00:21:27,288
If low perfusion persists, tubular cells can be injured. This is why
an initially prerenal problem can develop a structural component
rather than staying in one fixed category.

106
00:21:27,288 --> 00:21:41,064
Inflammatory glomerular or interstitial disease and nephrotoxic
exposures are other mechanisms. A recent medicine or infection is
evidence to explore, not enough to diagnose its exact renal effect.

107
00:21:41,064 --> 00:21:54,168
Rhabdomyolysis releases muscle contents, including myoglobin and
potassium. Consider prolonged compression, marked heat, seizures or
sustained strenuous muscle activity.

108
00:21:54,168 --> 00:22:06,264
Dark urine may reflect pigment, blood, concentration or another cause,
and some patients have none. Neither its presence nor absence
establishes the diagnosis alone.

109
00:22:06,264 --> 00:22:16,368
Obstruction can occur in the drainage system or at the bladder outlet.
Urine backs up and pressure can impair filtration if the problem is
significant enough.

110
00:22:16,368 --> 00:22:29,472
For global acute kidney dysfunction, bilateral obstruction, a single
functioning kidney or an outlet obstruction is especially relevant. A
unilateral stone with a healthy other kidney is a different situation.

111
00:22:29,472 --> 00:22:43,896
Ask about painful retention, the last normal void and an existing
catheter. Observe external drainage or obvious kinking if appropriate,
without breaking a closed system or manipulating internal components.

112
00:22:43,896 --> 00:22:57,504
Prompt definitive evaluation may be needed. Do not force oral water or
large IV volumes to push urine past a suspected blockage, and do not
invent a catheter procedure from the school mechanism lesson.

113
00:22:57,504 --> 00:23:10,488
The named published KDIGO criteria include a creatinine rise of at
least twenty-six point five micromoles per liter within forty-eight
hours, or at least one point five times baseline within seven days.

114
00:23:10,488 --> 00:23:22,680
Another route is urine output below point five milliliters per
kilogram per hour for six hours. These criteria use actual timed data,
not a casual report that the patient urinated less.

115
00:23:22,680 --> 00:23:34,728
You can suspect kidney risk and act on shock, obstruction or other
threats before these laboratory criteria are available. Do not guess a
baseline creatinine or wait six hours on scene.

116
00:23:34,728 --> 00:23:46,344
The official site currently still describes the twenty-twenty-six
update as a public-review draft preparing publication. Draft changes
are not presented here as an already final replacement.

117
00:23:46,344 --> 00:23:59,544
For a seventy-kilogram patient, point five milliliters per kilogram
per hour corresponds to thirty-five milliliters per hour. Over six
hours, that rate corresponds to two hundred ten milliliters.

118
00:23:59,544 --> 00:24:10,752
The criterion says below that rate over the required period. A single
small void or an incompletely emptied collection bag does not
establish the measured six-hour total.

119
00:24:10,752 --> 00:24:24,912
Check the start and finish times, measured amount, estimated mass and
whether collection was complete. If any are uncertain, communicate
that uncertainty instead of creating a precise diagnosis.

120
00:24:24,912 --> 00:24:37,296
A normal urine volume does not exclude AKI through the creatinine
pathway. Likewise, an oliguric patient does not automatically need an
extra two hundred ten milliliters of fluid.

121
00:24:37,296 --> 00:24:47,136
In this paper exercise, verified results show creatinine rising from
eighty-four to one hundred twelve micromoles per liter within forty-
eight hours.

122
00:24:47,136 --> 00:24:59,520
The increase is twenty-eight, which exceeds twenty-six point five. The
ratio is about one point three three, so the relative one-point-five
route is not needed to meet the absolute-change criterion.

123
00:24:59,520 --> 00:25:12,768
The criteria are alternatives, not three mandatory boxes. Use the
actual timing, units and clinical context, and distinguish a verified
laboratory record from a patient recollection.

124
00:25:12,768 --> 00:25:24,144
This is interpretation practice, not permission to claim a field
laboratory diagnosis without data. Current symptoms and the reason for
the change still need assessment.

125
00:25:24,144 --> 00:25:34,152
Establish what is normal for this patient: known kidney disease, usual
urine production, prescribed fluid limits and dialysis schedule when
relevant.

126
00:25:34,152 --> 00:25:45,936
Ask about vomiting, diarrhea, bleeding, poor intake, urinary
difficulty, fever and new pain. The reason for reduced urine may sit
outside the kidneys.

127
00:25:45,936 --> 00:25:58,800
Review new prescriptions, over-the-counter NSAIDs, diuretics,
potassium-containing supplements and recent exposures. Record the
timing and actual product rather than only the label kidney medicine.

128
00:25:58,800 --> 00:26:11,376
Breathlessness, swelling, generalized weakness, palpitations or
confusion can signal complications. None is specific, so assess the
whole patient and consider alternatives.

129
00:26:11,376 --> 00:26:23,880
A dehydrated patient with gastrointestinal losses may benefit from
indicated, carefully reassessed volume support. A congested patient
with weak cardiac output needs a different assessment.

130
00:26:23,880 --> 00:26:37,776
Edema and poor perfusion can coexist. Listen to breathing, assess work
and oxygenation, examine the circulation and compare the history
rather than classifying volume from swollen ankles alone.

131
00:26:37,776 --> 00:26:47,592
A blocked outlet may explain little urine without simple total-body
dehydration. More fluid cannot substitute for relief of the drainage
problem.

132
00:26:47,592 --> 00:26:59,304
The useful question is which mechanism is most likely and which
finding would change the plan. Multiple mechanisms or poor response
are reasons for consultation and reassessment.

133
00:26:59,304 --> 00:27:11,448
Use vascular access and fluids only when indicated through the actual
local pathway. Establish the baseline and the response you expect,
such as improved perfusion without worsening breathing.

134
00:27:11,448 --> 00:27:22,872
Administer the permitted amount and rate, then reassess. A low urine
report does not authorize an unlimited bolus or require completion of
a whole bag before transport.

135
00:27:22,872 --> 00:27:37,176
Worsening air movement, work of breathing, oxygenation or congestion
requires prompt review alongside the circulation. Failure to improve
pressure is not proof that another identical bolus is correct.

136
00:27:37,176 --> 00:27:49,848
Do not independently prescribe a diuretic, stop all long-term
medicines or operate dialysis equipment from a general textbook table.
Coordinate definitive care and protect continuity.

137
00:27:49,848 --> 00:28:02,784
Reduced renal excretion, muscle breakdown and shifts across cell
membranes can increase extracellular potassium. Ask about dialysis,
medicines, tissue injury and the actual course.

138
00:28:02,784 --> 00:28:14,952
Generalized weakness and abnormal rhythms may be warning signs, but
symptoms are often nonspecific. The laboratory confirms the
concentration when appropriate testing is available.

139
00:28:14,952 --> 00:28:28,992
Possible ECG clues include tented T waves, small or absent P waves, a
long PR and wide or bizarre complexes, including bradycardia. Do not
infer a precise potassium from one waveform.

140
00:28:28,992 --> 00:28:41,544
A normal-looking ECG does not exclude dangerous hyperkalemia, and the
changes need not follow a fixed staircase. Clinical context and
deterioration still require urgent evaluation.

141
00:28:41,544 --> 00:28:51,768
The treatment categories have different purposes. Calcium protects the
myocardium against potassium effects, but it does not remove potassium
from the body.

142
00:28:51,768 --> 00:29:03,648
Insulin-based or selected other therapies can shift potassium into
cells temporarily. Definitive removal may require dialysis or another
clinician-selected elimination approach.

143
00:29:03,648 --> 00:29:18,528
The current E zero three advanced pathway requires significant
instability, a compatible suspicion and consultation. A PCP
bronchodilator permission is not independent permission for a
potassium-shifting dose.

144
00:29:18,528 --> 00:29:32,832
At PCP level, address current airway and breathing needs, monitor,
obtain indicated access and arrange urgent advanced and definitive
care. Give a clear warning about the suspected mechanism and rhythm.

145
00:29:32,832 --> 00:29:43,896
A seventy-two-year-old with known heart failure and kidney disease
reports much less urine. Breathlessness and ankle swelling have
increased, and peripheral perfusion is poor.

146
00:29:43,896 --> 00:29:55,080
Do not label this simple dehydration from the urine report. Low
effective output and congestion can coexist, while obstruction or
another acute illness may add.

147
00:29:55,080 --> 00:30:09,720
Assess breathing and circulation, obtain useful rhythm information,
check glucose and ask about medicines, urinary symptoms and baseline
care. Consider potassium risk without assuming a concentration.

148
00:30:09,720 --> 00:30:20,736
Provide indicated support and seek an individualized care plan and
conveyance. A routine large fluid bolus or automatic drug bundle could
worsen this patient.

149
00:30:20,736 --> 00:30:32,952
A thirty-four-year-old has had prolonged agitation and heavy muscle
activity. Now there is marked heat, muscle pain and a report of dark
urine followed by much less output.

150
00:30:32,952 --> 00:30:45,192
Possible mechanisms include fluid loss, muscle breakdown and kidney
injury. Dark urine supports concern but does not prove myoglobin, and
the exact exposure remains uncertain.

151
00:30:45,192 --> 00:30:58,032
Continue safety, temperature management, breathing and circulation
support while arranging urgent evaluation. Assess rhythm and glucose
and anticipate potassium-related deterioration.

152
00:30:58,032 --> 00:31:09,816
Communicate the sequence rather than only the word intoxicated. The
receiving team needs the duration, heat, muscle findings, urine
change, measured observations and response.

153
00:31:09,816 --> 00:31:22,032
Reassessment begins with function. After airway support or naloxone,
check spontaneous depth and rate, air entry, oxygenation and the need
for ongoing assistance.

154
00:31:22,032 --> 00:31:32,928
After volume or positioning actions, compare perfusion and breathing
with the recorded baseline. A better pressure alone cannot outweigh
worsening respiratory function.

155
00:31:32,928 --> 00:31:45,360
Repeat relevant secretion, muscle, temperature and urine-history
checks. Some changes occur slowly, so use the actual timeline instead
of promising immediate renal recovery.

156
00:31:45,360 --> 00:31:57,576
If the predicted response does not occur, reconsider delivery failure,
co-exposure and another diagnosis. Update the receiving team when the
urgency or care requirement changes.

157
00:31:57,576 --> 00:32:08,616
Start with the current threat: inadequate ventilation, a contaminated
exposure, a widening-complex rhythm, severe heat or suspected renal
deterioration.

158
00:32:08,616 --> 00:32:19,008
Add the exposure and timeline or renal baseline and urine history.
Separate a measured result, a witness report and an inference about
the mechanism.

159
00:32:19,008 --> 00:32:30,000
Report exactly what was delivered, including prior responders, route,
dose, time and ventilation support. Then describe the actual change
and remaining problem.

160
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:43,176
Use working terms such as suspected opioid effect with persistent
sedation or possible mixed renal mechanisms. Clear uncertainty is more
useful than an unsupported definitive diagnosis.

161
00:32:43,176 --> 00:32:54,936
Explain why a wet, weak patient and a dry, delirious patient suggest
different receptor effects. Include the breathing threat and a
competing cause for each.

162
00:32:54,936 --> 00:33:04,560
Next explain the three renal categories using one cause each, then
show how prolonged hypoperfusion or sepsis can combine categories.

163
00:33:04,560 --> 00:33:15,264
For an original case, say your immediate safe action, the
authorization check and the response you expect. Do not answer only
with the drug or disease name.

164
00:33:15,264 --> 00:33:28,488
Finally state what would make you revise the plan, such as inadequate
ventilation, a widening rhythm, rising respiratory work or an exposure
hazard. This makes the explanation clinically usable.

165
00:33:28,488 --> 00:33:37,872
You should now compare the school toxidromes through secretions,
mental status, breathing, pupils, muscle effects, circulation and
temperature.

166
00:33:37,872 --> 00:33:48,264
You should explain why ventilation cannot wait for naloxone, why
adequate breathing is the endpoint and why contamination requires a
separate safe-access plan.

167
00:33:48,264 --> 00:34:01,656
For AKI, explain the three mechanisms, interpret genuine urine and
creatinine data, and recognize volume, potassium and acid-base
consequences without inventing treatment authority.

168
00:34:01,656 --> 00:34:12,768
Use the bilingual readings to review the mechanisms, then rehearse the
original cases aloud. Return to the missed mechanism when an answer is
only a memorized label.
